              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Sanskrit for an eyebrow, then the Sanskrit for the skin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: गुल्फः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "गुल्फः — gulphaḥ — \"an ankle\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gulpha \"the ankle\", also kulpha in the Rigveda, is perhaps borrowed from Dravidian: CDIAL 4216, after Burrow, takes the wavering k- and g- as a sign of that and compares a Dravidian word for \"hoof\". It lives on as Marathi gophā \"ankle\" (CDIAL 4216)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
